==================== Branch work278-float, patch #200 ====================

Document -mfloat16

When I added -mfloat16 to the PowerPC, I did not add it to rs6000.opt.urls.
This patch fixes that.

2026-11-26  Michael Meissner  <meissner@linux.ibm.com>

gcc/

	* config/rs6000/rs6000.opt.urls (-mfloat): Add switch.

==================== Branch work278-float, patch #200 ====================

Add 16-bit floating point support to the PowerPC

This patch adds support for two different 16-bit floating point formats to the
little endian PowerPC GCC server compilers.

This patch adds new options (-mfloat16 and -mno-float16) to control whether
16-bit floating point is enabled.  The 16-bit floating point support is only
supported on little endian 64-bit PowerPC systems.

This patch adds configuration options (--with-powerpc-float16 and
--without-powerpc-float16) to control whether 16-bit floating point is enabled.
On little endian 64-bit PowerPC Linux systems, 16-bit floating point is enabled
by default.

The two 16-bit floating point modes are passed and returned in traditional
floating point registers.

The scalar 16-bit floating point modes are passed and returned in the 16-bit
memory format (i.e. it is not converted to  the float/double scalar format).
Values are passed and returned in bits 48-63 of the traditional floating point
vector registers (i.e. the position that is loaded by the lxsihzx instruction,
stored by the stxsihzx instruction, and moved from a GPR via the mtvsrwz
instruction).  When a 16-bit value is passed or returned in a register, the
other bits in the register are unspecified.

The 128-bit vector 16-bit floating point modes are passed and returned like
other 128-bit vector modes (i.e. in the traditional Altivec vector registers).

If 16-bit floating point is enabled, the following macros are defined.

    __FLOAT16__		// _Float16 is available
    __BFLOAT16__	// __bf16 is available
    __FLOAT16_HW__	// Hardware conversions for _Float16 are used (power9)
    __BFLOAT16_HW__	// Hardware conversions for __bf16 are used (power10)


The two scalar 16-bit floating point modes are:


_Float16 (HFmode):
==================

This is the IEEE 754-2008 16-bit floating point.  It has 1 sign bit, 5
exponent bits, 10 explicit mantassia bits (the 11th bit is implied with
normalization).

The PowerPC ISA 3.0 (power9) has instructions to convert between the
scalar representations of _Float16 and float types.  The PowerPC ISA
3.1 (power10 and power11) has instructions for converting between the
even elements of _Float16 vectors and 32-bit float vectors.  In addition, the
MMA subsystem has support for _Float16 vector processing.


__bf16 (BFmode):
================

This is the brain 16-bit floating point created by the Google Brain
project.  It has 1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits, 7 explicit mantissa bits
(the 8th bit is implied with normalization).  The 16 bits in the
__bf16 format is the same as the upper 16 bits in the normal IEEE
754 32-bit floating point format.

The PowerPC ISA 3.1 (power10 and power11) has instructions for
converting between the even elements of __bf16 vectors and 32-bit float
vectors.  In addition, the MMA subsystem has support for __bf16.
vector processing.


In addition to basic support, this patch optimizes __bf16 binary and fma
operations.  Unlike _Float16 where we have instructions to convert between
HFmode and SFmode as scalar values, with BFmode, we only have vector
conversions.  Thus to do:

        __bf16 a, b, c;

        a = b + c;

the GCC compiler would have generated the following code:

	lhz          9,0(4)     // load __bf16 value b
	lhz          2,0(5)     // load __bf16 value c
	sldi         9,9,48     // prepare to call xscvspdpn
	sldi         2,2,48     // prepare to call xscvspdpn
	mtvsrd       12,9       // move to VSR register
	mtvsrd       11,2       // move to VSR register
	xscvspdpn    0,12       // convert to SF/DFmode scalar
	xscvspdpn    12,11      // convert to SF/DFmode scalar
	fadds        0,0,12     // SFmode add
	xscvdpsp     0,0        // convert to V4SF format
	xvcvspbf16   0,0        // convert to V8BF format
	stxsihx      0,0,3      // store b+c

This patch uses combiner patterns to now generate;

        lxsihzx      12,4,2     // load __bf16 value b
        lxsihzx      0,5,2      // load __bf16 value c
        xxspltw      12,12,1    // shift b into bits 16..31
        xxspltw      0,0,1      // shift c into bits 16..31
        xvcvbf16spn  12,12      // vector convert b into V4SFmode
        xvcvbf16spn  0,0        // vector convert c into V4SFmode
        xvaddsp      0,0,12     // vector b+c in V4SFmode
        xvcvspbf16   0,0        // convert b+c into BFmode memory format
        stxsihx      0,3,2      // store b+c

We cannot just define insns like 'addbf3' to keep the operation as
BFmode because GCC will not generate these patterns unless the user
uses -Ofast.  Without -Ofast, it will always convert BFmode into
SFmode.


The patch attempts to generate simple vectorization support for 16-bit floating
point.  This patch can generate vector instructions to convert the operation to
vector float operations for __bf16 using -O3, but it needs -Ofast to generate
the same vectorization for _Float16.


